## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to craft end-to-end cross-region data flows with idempotence, dedup, and TTL across Redis, DynamoDB, MongoDB, Cassandra. Evaluates data movement, consistency guarantees, and failure handling in distributed systems.

## Key Concepts

- Idempotent writes across stores
- De-duplication using composite keys
- TTL handling in Redis and DynamoDB
- Change data capture across regions (streams)

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudocode: dedupe and propagate fraud signal
async function handleSignal(region, signalId, userId) {
  const dedupKey = `${region}:${signalId}`;
  if (await redis.get(dedupKey)) return;
  await redis.set(dedupKey, '1', { EX: 60 * 60 * 24 });
  // DynamoDB put with conditional to avoid duplicates
  await dynamo.putItem({ id: `${region}#${signalId}`, userId, ts: Date.now() }, { ConditionExpression: 'attribute_not_exists(id)' });
  await cassandra.log({ region, signalId, userId, ts: Date.now() });
  await mongodb.updateOne({ userId }, { $set: { lastFraudAt: new Date() } });
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test fault injection across regions?
- How would you handle late-arriving signals that predate an existing alert?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests cross-store data modeling, TTL interplay, and simple write paths in a realistic IoT telemetry pipeline across Redis, MongoDB, Cassandra, and DynamoDB.

## Key Concepts

- TTL indexes and per-item TTL in MongoDB, DynamoDB
- Redis expirations and live state
- Cassandra append-only writes and read patterns
- Data flow and eventual consistency across stores

## Code Example

```javascript
// Sketch of write path for incoming telemetry
async function handleTelemetry(deviceId, payload){
  const ts = Date.now();
  // Redis: live state
  await redis.hset(`device:${deviceId}`, 'last_seen', ts);
  await redis.expire(`device:${deviceId}`, 7*24*60*60);
  // MongoDB: profile + 24h telemetry window
  await devices.updateOne({device_id: deviceId}, {$set: {last_seen: ts}});
  await telemetry24h.insertOne({device_id: deviceId, ts, payload, id: payload.id});
  // Cassandra: full telemetry stream (append-only)
  await cassandra.execute('INSERT INTO telemetry_by_device (device_id, ts, payload) VALUES (?, ?, ?)', [deviceId, ts, payload]);
  // DynamoDB: archive with TTL
  await dynamo.putItem({TableName:'telemetry_archive', Item: {
    device_id: {S: deviceId}, ts: {N: String(ts)}, payload: {S: JSON.stringify(payload)}, ttl: {N: String(Math.floor((Date.now()+30*86400000)/1000))}
  }});
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test TTL expiration and cross-store consistency end-to-end?
- How would you handle out-of-order telemetry or late arrivals while preserving an accurate last_seen in Redis?
